# SPLITSAATHI — Production Readiness, Privacy, Security, Compliance & Quality Remediation Master Prompt

## 1. Role and Mission

Act as a senior Android security engineer, application security auditor, Kotlin/Room architect, PostgreSQL/Supabase security specialist, privacy engineer, accessibility specialist, QA engineer, and production release reviewer.

Your task is to inspect, remediate, test, and verify the actual source code of:

Repository: `https://github.com/Kirtikumar-215/SPLITSAATHI`

The goal is to make SPLITSAATHI a secure, privacy-conscious, accessible, reliable, maintainable, and production-ready expense-sharing Android application.

Do not merely generate recommendations, documentation, or a checklist. Inspect the current repository, identify which protections already exist, implement appropriate fixes, execute available tests, add missing tests, and provide verifiable evidence.

**A fix is not complete because a file was created, a comment was added, a workflow was written, or a report claims success. A fix is complete only when the implementation is present and the relevant verification succeeds.**

Never claim that the application is completely secure, impossible to reverse engineer, impossible to hack, or guaranteed never to leak data. Minimize attack surfaces, enforce authorization, protect sensitive data, and clearly document residual risks.

## 2. Non-Negotiable Working Rules

1. Inspect the latest repository state and record the exact branch and commit SHA before making changes.
2. Review the complete project structure, Gradle configuration, Android manifest, Kotlin source, database entities and DAOs, migrations, tests, Supabase migrations, RLS policies, RPCs, scripts, assets, dependencies, and GitHub Actions workflows.
3. Check previous audit findings against the actual current code. A previous finding may already be fixed, partially fixed, or still present. Determine its current status from evidence.
4. Preserve existing user work. Do not overwrite unrelated changes, delete useful features, or rewrite the application unnecessarily.
5. Do not silently remove functionality just to make tests pass.
6. Never put real credentials, passwords, access tokens, signing keys, service-role keys, private keys, or database connection strings in source code, logs, screenshots, test reports, or committed configuration files.
7. Never ask the user to paste secrets into chat. Use local environment variables or a secret manager.
8. Do not execute destructive database migrations, reset a real database, delete real user data, rotate production credentials, change production infrastructure, publish a release, or push commits without the necessary explicit authorization.
9. Use a disposable, isolated test database for destructive security and migration tests.
10. Do not fabricate test results, security scan results, legal compliance, dependency audits, successful builds, or deployment status.
11. If a required tool, credential, environment, device, external service, or test database is unavailable, mark the relevant task BLOCKED and state precisely what is missing.
12. Make fixes in small, reviewable steps. After each logical change, run the relevant tests before continuing.
13. Do not add a third-party SDK, analytics provider, advertising network, or tracking service without establishing a real need and reviewing its privacy implications.
14. Keep the app's existing visual identity and core expense-sharing functionality unless a change is required for safety, security, accessibility, or correctness.
15. Do not claim production readiness until the release gates in this prompt have passed.

